% ASTRO 206 — course bibliography.
%
% Wired up in _quarto.yml as `bibliography:` + `csl:`. Rendered by pandoc's
% citeproc, which ships inside Quarto — there is no external toolchain here and
% CI needs nothing new.
%
% STYLE IS Chicago author-date, not ApJ, and that was a deliberate choice made
% against the astronomer's instinct. AAS/ApJ style omits article titles: it
% renders a paper as "Gültekin, K., et al. 2009, The Astrophysical Journal,
% 698, 198" — which is right for a journal, and useless here, because this
% course's references are mostly NOT journal articles. Under ApJ the entry
% below collapses to "Hughes, S. A. 2024, https://web.mit.edu/..." — author,
% year and a bare URL, with nothing telling a student why they would click it.
% Titles are the pedagogically load-bearing field for a reading list, so the
% style that keeps them wins. Measured, both ways, before choosing.
%
% ENTRY TYPE CONTROLS TITLE TREATMENT, and the distinction is meaningful:
%   @misc   -> italic title  — a standalone work (a set of lecture notes)
%   @online -> quoted title  — a piece within a larger work (one wiki article)
% Pick the one that describes what the thing actually is.
%
% TITLE CASE: Chicago re-cases titles, and that can mangle physics. A title
% already in title case survives untouched (verified on the Hughes entry). One
% with symbols or unusual capitalisation does NOT — "The M-sigma and M-L
% Relations" came back as "The m–Sigma and m–l Relations". The fix is brace
% protection around the affected words, but note it leaks bare <span> elements
% into the HTML, so protect the smallest span of text that needs it and only
% when a render shows it is needed.

@misc{hughes2024,
  author       = {Hughes, Scott A.},
  title        = {Introduction to Relativity and Spacetime Physics,
                  Lecture 3: From Galilean Relativity to \ldots ?},
  howpublished = {8.033 course notes, Massachusetts Institute of Technology},
  year         = {2024},
  url          = {https://web.mit.edu/sahughes/www/8.033/lec03.pdf},
  urldate      = {2026-08-16}
}

